{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"78c4679f87db9f76cb7c90acac106c118fba51c4058576511627e3cfefbee575","slug":"example","location":"/src/3-Libraries_and_Algorithms/3-Lecture-FFT/2-example.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Example Walkthrough: ( N = 8 )","content_includes_title":true,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"2-example.md","url":"/daje_website/build/2-example-ec912faf819baaa56ac59c635e73c832.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"plaintext","value":"Function four1(data, nn, isign)\n    Define constants:\n        PI = 3.141592653589793\n    Define variables:\n        n = nn * 2             // Total length of the data array (real + imaginary parts)\n        j = 1                   // Index for bit-reversed ordering\n        tempr, tempi            // Temporary variables for swapping and storing complex values\n\n    // Step 1: Bit-Reversal Permutation\n    For i from 1 to n-1 by 2 do\n        If j > i then\n            Swap data[j] and data[i]\n            Swap data[j+1] and data[i+1]\n        End If\n        \n        m = n / 2\n        While m >= 2 and j > m do\n            j = j - m\n            m = m / 2\n        End While\n        j = j + m\n    End For\n\n    // Step 2: Danielson-Lanczos Lemma Application\n    mmax = 2                    // Initial size of the transform (2-point)\n    While n > mmax do\n        istep = mmax * 2\n        theta = isign * (2 * PI / mmax)   // Angle for the complex exponential\n        wtemp = sin(0.5 * theta)\n        wpr = -2.0 * wtemp * wtemp\n        wpi = sin(theta)\n        wr = 1.0\n        wi = 0.0\n\n        // Step 2.1: Loop over different sub-transforms at the current stage\n        For m from 1 to mmax-1 by 2 do\n            // Step 2.2: Apply the transform to each element within the sub-transform\n            For i from m to n by istep do\n                j = i + mmax\n\n                // Compute temporary real and imaginary values\n                tempr = wr * data[j] - wi * data[j+1]\n                tempi = wr * data[j+1] + wi * data[j]\n\n                // Update data values for the current pair\n                data[j] = data[i] - tempr\n                data[j+1] = data[i+1] - tempi\n                data[i] = data[i] + tempr\n                data[i+1] = data[i+1] + tempi\n            End For\n\n            // Update wr and wi using recurrence relations\n            temp_wr = wr\n            wr = temp_wr * wpr - wi * wpi + wr\n            wi = wi * wpr + temp_wr * wpi + wi\n        End For\n\n        // Double the size of the transform for the next stage\n        mmax = istep\n    End While\nEnd Function","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"gnG92e3dJy"},{"type":"details","children":[{"type":"summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explanation first for loop","key":"GvZopWWVGS"}],"key":"RoUcrN6VYd"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first loop in the FFT code handles ","key":"TA5m3lpOui"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"bit-reversal reordering","key":"pXtLXASbhF"}],"key":"zMMDhUfh07"},{"type":"text","value":" of the input data, which is crucial for the FFT algorithm to compute the transformation in an efficient, in-place manner. For an FFT of size ","key":"qVFQHANTkb"},{"type":"span","class":"math-inline","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N = 8","key":"mlq1YInLgt"}],"key":"ClqufZSPmZ"},{"type":"text","value":", bit-reversal ordering means rearranging indices based on their binary representations, ","key":"m7nkPbNUju"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reversing the bits","key":"Yy8t4qUFSN"}],"key":"lRa8WoLibL"},{"type":"text","value":" to determine their new order. So, the index 1 (binary 001) would move to position 4 (binary 100), and so forth. This rearrangement helps the algorithm to structure data for efficient recursive application of smaller FFTs.","key":"yzEoXx5Z8P"}],"key":"fz7CbvH3vV"},{"type":"heading","depth":1,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example Walkthrough: ( N = 8 )","key":"aJBPkcp8Kv"}],"identifier":"example-walkthrough-n-8","label":"Example Walkthrough: ( N = 8 )","html_id":"example-walkthrough-n-8","implicit":true,"key":"TYquJ6NbqC"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suppose we have an array ","key":"Ykc6fa4Dot"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data","key":"doD9i9WOvM"},{"type":"text","value":" of length ( N = 8 ). In our case, the input data will be ","key":"GWQS2rquhx"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"f[0], f[1], ..., f[7]","key":"wqpRTKIJMK"},{"type":"text","value":". Since FFT operates on complex numbers, we consider ","key":"zRyHqzTBhi"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data","key":"PH1ZdsmR29"},{"type":"text","value":" with ","key":"G3cW20q9jv"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2*N","key":"SfZR3xXxhV"},{"type":"text","value":" real values, where each pair represents the real and imaginary parts for a point.","key":"ebQylk6gdc"}],"key":"OsNjIB3Jwc"},{"type":"heading","depth":1,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial Setup","key":"TkOMmfbspP"}],"identifier":"initial-setup","label":"Initial Setup","html_id":"initial-setup","implicit":true,"key":"ztEZS2IqhB"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Given:","key":"oSbDol30Mt"}],"key":"j5iBp2oL01"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":null,"spread":false,"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":false,"checked":null,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"n = 2 * nn = 2 * 8 = 16","key":"WW4vK6ix1v"},{"type":"text","value":" (total array length in terms of real + imaginary parts)","key":"ni6m1inX5B"}],"key":"VWuMnTRHCh"}],"key":"o3HE31AmBw"},{"type":"listItem","spread":false,"checked":null,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"j = 1","key":"XobWunNfX4"},{"type":"text","value":" (index for tracking bit-reversed positions)","key":"OBWbign